In a telephone interview with the Research Directorate, the AFJCI secretary general stated that Ivorian legislation has [translation] \'93no specific law on domestic violence\'94
 (AFJCI 30 Nov. 2015). Similarly, in its report submitted to the UN Human Rights Committee in 2013, C\'f4te d\rquote Ivoire recognizes that [C\'f4te d\rquote Ivoire English version] \'93there is no specific law against domestic violence\'94
 and notes that this constitutes a \'93gap\'94 in the Ivorian legislation with regard to protecting women against violence (C\'f4te d\rquote Ivoire 21 May 2013, para. 164). The same source indicates that [C\'f4te d\rquote Ivoire English version] \'93
[t]he only provisions that can be invoked [in matters of domestic violence] are the general provisions on assault in the Criminal Code\'94 (ibid., para. 131). }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid9323967 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid9310748\charrsid9310748 The LIDHO representative, however, added that the provisions of the Criminal Code relating to murder and violence or 
death threats could also be invoked in matters of domestic violence (LIDHO 2 Dec. 2015), while the AFJCI secretary general explained that domestic violence was also punished under the provision on rape in the Criminal Code (AFJCI 30 Nov. 2015). Sources in
dicate that the law does not specifically penalize spousal rape (US 25 June 2015, 16-17; Freedom House 28 Jan. 2015). In its concluding observations on the initial report of C\'f4te d\rquote 
Ivoire regarding the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights, the UN Human Rights Committee states it was concerned by the fact that [UN English version] \'93article 354 [of the C\'f4te d\rquote 
Ivoire Criminal Code], which criminalizes rape, fails to mention marital rape\'94 (28 Apr. 2015, para. 13). Article 354 of the Criminal Code states the following:
\par Rape is punishable by imprisonment of 5 to 20 years.
\par It is punishable by life imprisonment if the perpetrator
\par 1. Committed the crime with the assistance of one or more persons, or
\par 2. Is the father, a relative or a person with authority over the victim, who is responsible for the victim\rquote s education or their intellectual or professional training
\par It is also punishable by life imprisonment if the victim is a minor of 15 years of age. (C\'f4te d\rquote Ivoire 1981, Art. 354)
\par A training module on marriage and divorce, created in 2013 by a lawyer affiliated with the AFJCI, indicates that violence against a husband or wife, in any form, constitutes legal grounds for a [translation] \'93fault-based divorce,\'94
 which does not require the mutual consent of the spouses (AFJCI 2013, 19-21). Similarly, the report submitted by the government of C\'f4te d\rquote Ivoire to the UN Human Rights Committee states that [C\'f4te d\rquote Ivoire English version] \'93
[f]ault-based divorce is allowed in cases of ... excesses, abuse or serious insult of one spouse by the other\'94 (C\'f4te d\rquote Ivoire 21 May 2013, para. 139). The AFJCI training module explains that [translation] \'93
a single transgression is sufficient if it is serious enough\'94 and that \'93the spouse filing for divorce has the onus of proving the other is at fault\'94
 (AFJCI 2013, 20). Corroborating information could not be found among the sources consulted by the Research Directorate within the time constraints of this Response.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs24\i\lang9\langfe2057\super\langnp9\insrsid9310748 
